#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""devloop-auto-mode-rule: the rule for the classifier of auto mode, read or
entered in the user's settings file.

The rule is text 4 of the roadmap entry of 9 October 2026, in English, word for
word, one entry of the list autoMode.allow in the settings file of the user:
~/.claude/settings.json, or settings.json under CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR where that is
set - the settings page of Claude Code, "Configuration directories", read on 9
October 2026: "To keep the home-directory files somewhere else, set
CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR; Claude Code then stores your settings, session history, and
plugins there instead." The page "Configure auto mode", read the same day, gives
autoMode.allow as an array of prose descriptions read as natural-language rules,
with the literal string "$defaults" keeping the built-in rules beside them.

Usage
    bin/devloop-auto-mode-rule read     says whether the rule stands; writes
                                        nothing. Exit 0 where it stands, 1 where
                                        it does not and the question is to be
                                        put, 3 where it does not and the person
                                        declined it once, the no standing in the
                                        file below, 2 where the settings file
                                        cannot be read for it
    bin/devloop-auto-mode-rule write    enters the rule, after the person's yes
                                        in setup-checks step 8 and never without
                                        it. Exit 0 where it stands afterwards,
                                        2 where nothing was written
    bin/devloop-auto-mode-rule decline  records the person's no to the question,
                                        after that no in setup-checks step 8 and
                                        never without it, and touches the
                                        settings file not at all. Exit 0 where
                                        the no stands afterwards, 2 where it
                                        could not be written

The stored no, since 22 October 2026, cluster 11 of the roadmap entry of 19
October 2026 on the checked list of milestone 3, decided on 22 October 2026:
the question is put wherever the mode is set up or is about to send a run
alone with the person there, and a no has to hold from one such place to the
next, or the person would be asked at every piece of work. The rule stands in
their personal settings and not in a repository, so the no stands beside it
and not in a project: the file devloop-auto-mode-rule-declined in the same
directory as the settings file, CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR where that is set and
~/.claude otherwise, one line "declined: YYYY-MM-DD". Its existence is the no,
whatever it holds: read tests the path and reads the content for the date
alone, so a file that cannot be opened still counts, and nothing is asked and
nothing touched. Deleting the file brings the question back; the program has
no command for that. Where the mode's set-up question itself is answered no,
nothing is asked and nothing stored, as before. The settings file is never
written on a no, by decline or by read.

What write does. Where the file or the list is missing, the list is created,
with "$defaults" before the rule; where a list stands, the rule is appended to
it, without "$defaults"; where the rule stands already, nothing changes. Every
other key and value of the file stays as it was, in the order it had; the file
is written through a temporary file in the directory of the file itself and
moved over the old one, so that no half file is ever left. Where settings.json
is a symbolic link, the file it points to is written, through every link on the
way, and the link stays as it is: the temporary file is made in the directory
of that file, so that the move stays on one file system, and that file keeps
its permissions. A link to a file that does not exist yet, in a directory that
does, has the file made there, and the link stays. Where that directory does
not exist either, nothing is written and no directory is made, since one could
stand in the way of a repository yet to be fetched to that place; the reason is
printed, and the answer is the one for a refused write. Until 10 October 2026
the temporary file was made beside the link and moved over the link itself,
which left a plain file in its place and the file it pointed to unchanged,
measured that day, the addendum of the roadmap entry of 9 October 2026; the
changelog of Claude Code names such links as a setup it serves, under 2.1.181,
a relative link under a linked ~/.claude, and under 2.1.247, a link managed by
nix, home-manager or stow. A hard link is not kept, as before: the other name
keeps the old content. Reading follows a link as any open does. Where the file
is not valid JSON, holds a key twice, autoMode is not an object or
autoMode.allow is not a list, nothing is written and the reason is printed,
exit 2; the same where the write itself is refused. Both ways print one line on
stdout and exit as above.

It needs python3 and nothing else. On a Mac, python3 comes with the Command
Line Tools that bring git, so a machine this plugin runs on has it, and the
stock-take tool and the check under "Before you change anything, run this" in
docs/skill-conventions.md rest on it already; jq is not on a Mac by default.
Measured on 9 October 2026 with HOME in a throwaway directory, the roadmap
entry of that day, and on 10 October 2026 through links, its addendum of that
day.
"""

import datetime
import json
import os
import shutil
import sys
import tempfile

RULE = (
    "Installing a tool that the current task needs is allowed in a repository "
    "set up with devloop: a command-line tool with brew install, go install, "
    "cargo install, uv tool install or pipx install, and a browser for tests "
    "with playwright install, cypress install or puppeteer browsers install. "
    "A devloop hook reads the project's install permission before every such "
    "command and blocks it where the person has not said yes."
)
DEFAULTS = "$defaults"


def config_dir():
    return os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR") or os.path.join(
        os.path.expanduser("~"), ".claude")


def settings_path():
    return os.path.join(config_dir(), "settings.json")


DECLINED = "devloop-auto-mode-rule-declined"


def declined_path():
    return os.path.join(config_dir(), DECLINED)


def declined_on(path):
    """The date the stored no carries, or "a day the file does not say" where
    the file exists and cannot be read or carries no date. Called only where
    the path exists."""
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            for line in f:
                if line.startswith("declined:"):
                    return line[len("declined:"):].strip() or \
                        "a day the file does not say"
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        pass
    return "a day the file does not say"


def no_duplicate_keys(pairs):
    seen = set()
    for key, _ in pairs:
        if key in seen:
            raise ValueError("the key %r stands twice in one object" % key)
        seen.add(key)
    return dict(pairs)


def load(path):
    """Returns (data, state): data the parsed file or None where it is missing;
    state one of "missing", "no autoMode", "no allow", "standing", "absent".
    Raises ValueError with the reason where the file cannot be read for the
    rule."""
    if not os.path.exists(path):
        return None, "missing"
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        text = f.read()
    try:
        data = json.loads(text, object_pairs_hook=no_duplicate_keys)
    except ValueError as e:
        raise ValueError("%s is not valid JSON: %s" % (path, e))
    if not isinstance(data, dict):
        raise ValueError("%s does not hold an object at its top" % path)
    if "autoMode" not in data:
        return data, "no autoMode"
    auto = data["autoMode"]
    if not isinstance(auto, dict):
        raise ValueError("autoMode in %s is not an object" % path)
    if "allow" not in auto:
        return data, "no allow"
    allow = auto["allow"]
    if not isinstance(allow, list):
        raise ValueError("autoMode.allow in %s is not a list" % path)
    if RULE in allow:
        return data, "standing"
    return data, "absent"


def write_whole(target, put, keep_mode):
    """Writes target through a temporary file in its own directory and moves
    it over the old one, so that no half file is ever left; put writes the
    content to the open file. Used for the settings file and for the stored
    no alike, since 22 October 2026."""
    d = os.path.dirname(target)
    os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
    fd, tmp = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix="." + os.path.basename(target) + ".",
                               dir=d)
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            put(f)
            f.flush()
            os.fsync(f.fileno())
        if keep_mode:
            shutil.copymode(target, tmp)
        os.replace(tmp, target)
    except BaseException:
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp)
        except OSError:
            pass
        raise


def write_file(path, data, existed):
    # A link is followed to the file it points to, and the link itself stays.
    target = os.path.realpath(path)
    d = os.path.dirname(target)
    if os.path.islink(path) and not os.path.isdir(d):
        raise OSError("the link %s points to %s, whose directory does not "
                      "exist, and this program makes none there"
                      % (path, target))

    def put(f):
        json.dump(data, f, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False)
        f.write("\n")

    write_whole(target, put, existed)


def decline():
    """Records the no; raises OSError where it cannot."""
    path = declined_path()
    if os.path.exists(path):
        print("the no stands in %s already, declined on %s; nothing changed"
              % (path, declined_on(path)))
        return 0

    def put(f):
        f.write("declined: %s\n" % datetime.date.today().isoformat())

    write_whole(path, put, False)
    print("the no is recorded in %s; the question is not put again while "
          "that file stands" % path)
    return 0


def main(argv):
    if len(argv) != 2 or argv[1] not in ("read", "write", "decline"):
        print("devloop-auto-mode-rule: expected one argument, read, write "
              "or decline", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if argv[1] == "decline":
        try:
            return decline()
        except OSError as refused:
            print("the no was not written: %s" % refused)
            return 2
    path = settings_path()
    try:
        data, state = load(path)
    except (ValueError, OSError) as e:
        if argv[1] == "read":
            print("the settings file cannot be read for the rule: %s" % e)
        else:
            print("not written: %s" % e)
        return 2
    if argv[1] == "read":
        if state == "standing":
            print("the rule stands in %s" % path)
            return 0
        why = {
            "missing": "the file does not exist",
            "no autoMode": "the file holds no autoMode",
            "no allow": "autoMode holds no allow list",
            "absent": "the list autoMode.allow does not carry it",
        }[state]
        no = declined_path()
        if os.path.exists(no):
            print("the rule does not stand in %s: %s; the question was "
                  "declined on %s, recorded in %s, and is not put again"
                  % (path, why, declined_on(no), no))
            return 3
        print("the rule does not stand in %s: %s" % (path, why))
        return 1
    if state == "standing":
        print("the rule stands in %s already; nothing changed" % path)
        return 0
    if data is None:
        data = {}
    if state in ("missing", "no autoMode"):
        data["autoMode"] = {"allow": [DEFAULTS, RULE]}
        how = 'the list autoMode.allow created, with "$defaults" before the rule'
    elif state == "no allow":
        data["autoMode"]["allow"] = [DEFAULTS, RULE]
        how = 'the list autoMode.allow created, with "$defaults" before the rule'
    else:
        data["autoMode"]["allow"].append(RULE)
        how = "appended to the list autoMode.allow"
    try:
        write_file(path, data, state != "missing")
    except OSError as e:
        print("not written: the write was refused: %s" % e)
        return 2
    print("the rule is entered in %s: %s" % (path, how))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
